WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.560
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone.
I'm Ashish Kapoor,

00:00:25.561 --> 00:00:26.970
and I welcome you on behalf of

00:00:26.971 --> 00:00:29.910
the AirSim Team at
Microsoft Research.

00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:31.830
So, the recent successes in

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.450
machine learning and
reinforcement learning

00:00:33.451 --> 00:00:34.535
have been very impressive,

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:37.390
but they've been
limited to scenarios,

00:00:37.391 --> 00:00:40.475
such as Arcade Games and
Game of Go, et cetera.

00:00:40.476 --> 00:00:43.575
Typically, closed
world situations,

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.250
where your AI agent
doesn't need to worry

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:49.225
about the exogenous factors,

00:00:49.226 --> 00:00:53.065
such as when pedestrians
or the cars, et cetera.

00:00:53.066 --> 00:00:56.030
Consequently, we,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.600
at the AirSim team have been
thinking about how to take

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:02.460
these techniques and apply
them to real-world tasks,

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:03.845
such as autonomous driving,

00:01:03.846 --> 00:01:06.390
flight, robotics, et cetera.

00:01:06.391 --> 00:01:09.860
Consequently, the key idea
that we've been working on

00:01:09.861 --> 00:01:14.490
is to create these
high-fidelity simulations,

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:16.250
which are essentially
video game on

00:01:16.251 --> 00:01:20.140
steroids that are played by
autonomous system agents.

00:01:20.141 --> 00:01:24.500
So, the whole idea being that and

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.110
AI pipeline if it
doesn't access to

00:01:27.111 --> 00:01:30.435
a simulated environment in
which it can take actions,

00:01:30.436 --> 00:01:33.835
and if the such a
simulator environment has

00:01:33.836 --> 00:01:37.690
high-fidelity then we can
hope to train AI systems,

00:01:37.691 --> 00:01:39.820
which then we can
deploy in real world.

00:01:39.821 --> 00:01:40.980
So, what I'll do is,

00:01:40.981 --> 00:01:44.380
I'll ask Shital Shah who's
the Lead Architect for

00:01:44.381 --> 00:01:46.175
our AirSim pipeline to

00:01:46.176 --> 00:01:48.565
give you a little bit more
details about AirSim.

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:50.660
&gt;&gt; Thank you Ashish. So, we

00:01:50.661 --> 00:01:52.010
want to show you
some of the features

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:55.730
that we have built and what
you can do with AirSim.

00:01:55.731 --> 00:01:59.590
So, AirSim is open-source
cross-platform project.

00:01:59.591 --> 00:02:01.515
So, you can get it right now,

00:02:01.516 --> 00:02:02.915
there are the releases,

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:04.460
where you can download binaries,

00:02:04.461 --> 00:02:05.975
you can start playing
with right away.

00:02:05.976 --> 00:02:09.165
It works on Windows,
as well as the Linux.

00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:12.165
Here are some of the
things you can do with it.

00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:16.100
So, AirSim provides you
all this ground truth,

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:17.900
with depth view
segmentation view,

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:20.665
which is very hard to
get in real world,

00:02:20.666 --> 00:02:23.910
but you can obtain in
simulation very nicely.

00:02:23.911 --> 00:02:27.335
So, you can do lot of computer
vision and learning task.

00:02:27.336 --> 00:02:29.965
We have built drones simulation,

00:02:29.966 --> 00:02:31.300
as well as car simulation.

00:02:31.301 --> 00:02:32.330
So, here you can see

00:02:32.331 --> 00:02:35.850
the city environment and
you can see this big city,

00:02:35.851 --> 00:02:38.660
this is related to big city
that we have built.

00:02:38.661 --> 00:02:45.405
This is another environment
from Hawaii Hana Highway,

00:02:45.406 --> 00:02:47.600
where you can drive

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:50.270
the car and you can test
your self-driving algorithms,

00:02:50.271 --> 00:02:52.630
and you can do lot
of detection and

00:02:52.631 --> 00:02:55.100
object recognition
stuff, for example,

00:02:55.101 --> 00:02:58.040
identifying animals,
identifying objects,

00:02:58.041 --> 00:03:01.915
and you can also do a lot
of machine learning,

00:03:01.916 --> 00:03:04.910
reinforcement learning
work in AirSim,

00:03:04.911 --> 00:03:08.204
where you can build
your reward function

00:03:08.205 --> 00:03:13.730
and try out ideal algorithms
in the open worlds.

00:03:13.731 --> 00:03:18.660
So, this is a great resource
platform for reinforcement.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:24.750
There are some examples stuff
we've been trying to do.

00:03:24.751 --> 00:03:28.490
For example here, car is trying

00:03:28.491 --> 00:03:33.665
to learn how to drive
without collisions.

00:03:33.666 --> 00:03:36.920
So, with saying that,

00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:40.590
let me move over to my colleague,

00:03:40.591 --> 00:03:44.865
Jim, and he can show you
more about the environments.

00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:47.680
&gt;&gt; Thanks Shital. As
Shital indicated,

00:03:47.681 --> 00:03:49.420
I'm Jim Piavis and
I'm a member on

00:03:49.421 --> 00:03:51.680
the AirSim team at MS Research.

00:03:51.681 --> 00:03:53.720
So, AirSim actually
supports a number of

00:03:53.721 --> 00:03:56.595
different environments and
since we build on Unreal 4,

00:03:56.596 --> 00:03:59.120
it's a relatively common
skillset that we can actually go

00:03:59.121 --> 00:04:00.260
out and find on the open market

00:04:00.261 --> 00:04:01.710
actually build our environments.

00:04:01.711 --> 00:04:03.540
The nice thing is that,

00:04:03.541 --> 00:04:05.180
we can rapidly build these out

00:04:05.181 --> 00:04:06.950
and it just depends
on the complexity.

00:04:06.951 --> 00:04:09.140
As Shital indicated and he
showed a little bit earlier,

00:04:09.141 --> 00:04:11.885
we have a number of different
environments that we use.

00:04:11.886 --> 00:04:13.490
These were all constructed using

00:04:13.491 --> 00:04:17.250
typical modeling tools
such as 3D Max,

00:04:17.251 --> 00:04:19.620
Maya, whatever it takes basically

00:04:19.621 --> 00:04:23.925
for 3D animation and creation.

00:04:23.926 --> 00:04:25.100
What you're looking at now is,

00:04:25.101 --> 00:04:26.510
a warehouse
environment we did for

00:04:26.511 --> 00:04:28.820
Toyota materials handling for

00:04:28.821 --> 00:04:31.050
the Hannover Messe show
several months ago.

00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:33.200
It shows several different drones

00:04:33.201 --> 00:04:34.550
that Toyota was working on and

00:04:34.551 --> 00:04:36.350
how those might be visualized

00:04:36.351 --> 00:04:38.220
within that warehouse
environment.

00:04:38.221 --> 00:04:40.340
We can apply
reinforcement learning

00:04:40.341 --> 00:04:42.950
to each of the hero actors within

00:04:42.951 --> 00:04:45.300
the scene as they teach
themselves how to

00:04:45.301 --> 00:04:47.730
actually maneuver
within the warehouse.

00:04:47.731 --> 00:04:49.790
This was probably
constructed I think

00:04:49.791 --> 00:04:50.540
it took a total of

00:04:50.541 --> 00:04:52.070
about three and half weeks
and a couple of

00:04:52.071 --> 00:04:53.520
people to actually build this and

00:04:53.521 --> 00:04:55.220
it originated on a set of

00:04:55.221 --> 00:05:00.070
CAD drawings provided to
us from the customer.

00:05:00.071 --> 00:05:02.370
This is a little bit more complex

00:05:02.371 --> 00:05:04.900
for those that have
been in Building 99.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.470
This is the interior or

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:08.420
at least the lower floor
of Building 99,

00:05:08.421 --> 00:05:09.890
which involves both the atrium,

00:05:09.891 --> 00:05:12.165
the cafe, some of
the office areas,

00:05:12.166 --> 00:05:15.475
and it's a relatively
complex environment.

00:05:15.476 --> 00:05:18.070
This environment had to
be entirely hand created,

00:05:18.071 --> 00:05:21.210
but what we used was a drone to

00:05:21.211 --> 00:05:25.215
capture roughly 400 to
500 images of the atrium.

00:05:25.216 --> 00:05:28.000
Then we reconstructed
a point cloud using

00:05:28.001 --> 00:05:29.270
photogrammetry and used that

00:05:29.271 --> 00:05:31.635
as very accurate
reference material.

00:05:31.636 --> 00:05:34.010
Then after handing that
off to our art team,

00:05:34.011 --> 00:05:35.570
they're able to
actually reconstruct

00:05:35.571 --> 00:05:38.645
the entire scene over the course
of about three weeks.

00:05:38.646 --> 00:05:40.730
I think we had
three to four folks

00:05:40.731 --> 00:05:42.620
working on this at
any given time,

00:05:42.621 --> 00:05:44.755
and it actually turned out to be

00:05:44.756 --> 00:05:47.870
quite a nice little environment.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.310
Shital also showed
the city environment

00:05:52.311 --> 00:05:53.765
we've built an entire city,

00:05:53.766 --> 00:05:56.150
which includes
vehicles, pedestrians,

00:05:56.151 --> 00:05:57.770
which can be varied
place to place,

00:05:57.771 --> 00:06:00.280
we have whether
particle effects that can

00:06:00.281 --> 00:06:03.650
be configured within the scene,

00:06:03.651 --> 00:06:07.160
and we've also been

00:06:07.161 --> 00:06:10.550
able to support the latest
Microsoft Build Conference,

00:06:10.551 --> 00:06:12.810
which was held about a month
and a half ago.

00:06:12.811 --> 00:06:15.620
We had an interesting
activity for

00:06:15.621 --> 00:06:23.380
the developers to construct
an AI application,

00:06:23.381 --> 00:06:26.480
leveraging cognitive services
to identify objects.

00:06:26.481 --> 00:06:29.240
In this case, what we have is

00:06:29.241 --> 00:06:33.570
real-world objects
in the upper right.

00:06:33.571 --> 00:06:36.340
The upper left is the synthetic
images, which we built.

00:06:36.341 --> 00:06:38.750
Those were placed on
a soccer field and then we

00:06:38.751 --> 00:06:42.070
basically flew a drone within
the AirSim application,

00:06:42.071 --> 00:06:45.260
collecting visual data, which
was then sourced through

00:06:45.261 --> 00:06:48.795
to cognitive services for
object identification.

00:06:48.796 --> 00:06:50.720
In the end, we were actually able

00:06:50.721 --> 00:06:53.300
to achieve about
an 85 percent hit rate

00:06:53.301 --> 00:06:55.640
between the training we

00:06:55.641 --> 00:06:58.260
sourced on synthetic objects
versus the real-world,

00:06:58.261 --> 00:07:01.010
and we think this is very
promising in the future,

00:07:01.011 --> 00:07:03.470
where we can use
synthetic environments to

00:07:03.471 --> 00:07:06.905
train in for external.

00:07:06.906 --> 00:07:10.350
Finally, we do have environments
we used for drones,

00:07:10.351 --> 00:07:11.510
this was actually somewhat of

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:14.890
an out-of-the-box scenario
with mountains,

00:07:14.891 --> 00:07:17.810
but what we did is
augmented with power lines,

00:07:17.811 --> 00:07:20.195
so that we can start
teaching the drone through

00:07:20.196 --> 00:07:22.235
reinforcement learning
how to actually stay

00:07:22.236 --> 00:07:24.460
on the power lines as
you see in a scene.

00:07:24.461 --> 00:07:27.560
So, we're using
a combination of say,

00:07:27.561 --> 00:07:29.030
out of the box, which
are quickly sourced,

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:30.530
we can grab those and then if we

00:07:30.531 --> 00:07:32.360
need to do any custom
modifications,

00:07:32.361 --> 00:07:33.470
we can do that with the scene.

00:07:33.471 --> 00:07:36.099
So, the scenes are
very extensible,

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:37.340
they're easy to create,

00:07:37.341 --> 00:07:39.285
we can get things turned
around pretty quick.

00:07:39.286 --> 00:07:42.050
So, hopefully, this is
a pretty good primer

00:07:42.051 --> 00:07:45.540
on what we're doing with
AirSim. Thank you very much.

